   "text": "I read the skeleton as a 3\u2011methoxy\u201117\u2011methylmorphinan, the same ring system as dextromethorphan/levomethorphan, though the drawing is laid out unusually. I traced the atoms and rings one by one, then assigned the three stereocentres from the wedges and hash.\n\n**Skeleton**\n- **Aromatic ring:** six carbons with alternating double bonds, carrying an OMe group on the carbon at the top right.\n- **Aromatic attachments:** one ring carbon is bonded to the quaternary carbon, and the adjacent aromatic carbon is bonded to a CH2.\n- **Piperidine ring:** N(CH3) joins a CH2\u2013CH2 chain that leads to the quaternary carbon. The N also bonds to a CH that carries an H wedge.\n- **Middle ring:** the N\u2011bearing CH is bonded to a CH2 that attaches to the aromatic ring, and to a second CH that carries an H wedge. That second CH is bonded to the quaternary carbon.\n- **Cyclohexane ring:** the quaternary carbon and the second CH are joined through four CH2 groups.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- Both CH\u2013H bonds are drawn as wedges.\n- The quaternary carbon has a hashed bond to the CH2 of the CH2\u2013CH2\u2013N chain.\n- Working out the chirality from the 2D geometry gives `[C@@]` for the quaternary carbon, `[C@@H]` for the N\u2011bearing CH and `[C@H]` for the other CH, in the atom order of the SMILES below.\n\n**Check:** the SMILES has three rings besides the aromatic one (piperidine, the ring closed through the CH2 on the aromatic ring, and the cyclohexane), all closed correctly.\n\nSMILES: COc1ccc2c(c1)[C@@]34CCN(C)[C@@H](C2)[C@H]3CCCC4"
